# Pinned revision of the cross-repo counterpart in the other direction:
# libviprs/acadsharp-rs-tests, the suite that exercises this crate through
# `acadsharp-rs = { path = "../acadsharp-rs" }`.
#
# The `Suite (acadsharp-rs-tests)` job in .github/workflows/ci.yml lays that
# suite down beside this checkout at exactly this commit and runs it against
# the tree the PR is proposing. It fetches one sha. It never guesses a
# same-named branch and never falls back to the default branch, which is what
# `libviprs`' integration job does and what libviprs#1013 is open about.
#
# Shape: comment lines, then exactly one 40-character lowercase sha, and
# nothing else. The first non-comment, non-blank line is authoritative, so a
# sha quoted inside a comment is never picked up by mistake. A branch name here
# is always wrong. `tests/suite_pin_check.rs` asserts all of that.
#
# The rule for which commit may go here is the mirror of the suite's own
# COUNTERPART_REV rule, and it is deliberately looser in one direction:
#
#   - On a PR branch, this may name a suite commit that is not on the suite's
#     `main` (the head of an open suite PR). A breaking crate change needs the
#     matching suite change to exist before either side can merge, so forbidding
#     it would make paired changes impossible. The job prints a `::notice`.
#   - On `main`, that same pin fails the job. A red `Suite (acadsharp-rs-tests)`
#     on `main` means exactly one thing: the one-line follow-up PR that moves
#     this file onto the suite's merge commit is owed. The landing order for a
#     paired change is written out in README.md.
#
# To bump: set this to an acadsharp-rs-tests commit sha and say why in a comment
# above, the way this one does.
#
# The merge commit of libviprs/acadsharp-rs-tests#16 (H1.1), landed 2026-09-13.
# That is the commit where the suite grew `acadsharp-rs = { path =
# "../acadsharp-rs" }`, its own `COUNTERPART_REV`, the `clone-counterpart`
# action and `tests/counterpart_pin.rs`, so it is the first suite commit this
# job can do real work against. `compare/main...` reads `identical` today, so
# the merged-only rule passes here and would pass on `main` too.
